  m_Name: lantern_hill
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::Data.ScriptableObjects.StoryBookDefinition
  Id: lantern_hill
  Title: The Lantern Under the Hill
  Author: Ember
  Color: {r: 1, g: 0.7, b: 0.35, a: 1}
  Hint: Deep in the crystal cave, beneath the southern hills
  Pages:
  - 'Beneath the southern hills there is a cave where the crystals hum. Long ago
    a fox spirit lived there, all alone.


    She was made of warm light, the way
    other foxes are made of fur, and her tail flickered like a candle. She had come
    to the cave in a snowstorm, many lifetimes before, and stayed.'
  - 'On the night of that storm a traveller had been lost in the hills. The fox spirit
    had seen his little shape wandering in circles in the snow, and she had called
    and called, but she was too far away and too faint to be seen.


    In the morning
    he found his own way home, cold but safe. Still, she never forgot watching him
    wander, and being no help at all.'
  - 'So she made a lantern. She chose the clearest crystal in the cave, hollowed
    it out with her teeth, and breathed a little of her own flame inside.


    She
    hung it at the mouth of the cave, where it could be seen from every path in the
    hills.


    "There," she said. "Nobody will ever be lost in the dark again.
    Not while I''m here."'
  - 'For a while, travellers came. A shepherd in the fog. A pedlar with a broken
    cartwheel. Two sisters who had been arguing about the way and were both wrong.


    They
    followed the lantern, sheltered in the cave until morning, and went on their
    way with a thank you. The fox spirit was glad of them, though none of them ever
    stayed long.'
  - 'Then the village built a new road, wide and paved, that went around the hills
    instead of over them.


    Years passed, and nobody came.


    The fox spirit
    kept the lantern burning anyway. But a lantern lit with your own flame takes
    a little of you every night, and nobody was giving any back. Her flame grew smaller,
    and so did she.'
  - 'In the end she was hardly more than a flicker, curled up beside the lantern,
    barely able to lift her head.


    Then one evening she heard a clatter of hooves,
    a bleat, and a very determined voice shouting, "Biscuit! Biscuit, you come back
    here this minute!"


    A goat trotted into the cave. Close behind it came a
    small girl with a muddy hem.'
  - 'The girl caught Biscuit by the collar. Then she saw the lantern, and forgot
    all about the goat.


    "Oh," she said. "Oh, it''s beautiful. Is it yours?"


    "It
    is," whispered the fox spirit. "Are you lost?"


    The girl looked puzzled.
    "No. I live just over the hill. I can see the light from my window. I''ve always
    wanted to know what it was."'
  - 'Her name was Rosa, and she stayed until the stars came out, asking questions.
    Did the crystals really hum? (They did.) Did spirits eat supper? (Not usually,
    but she would try anything once.)


    The next day Rosa came back with bread
    and honey. The day after, she brought her brother. By the end of the summer,
    half the village children knew the way to the cave.'
  - 'With every visitor the fox spirit''s flame grew a little brighter, until she
    glowed so warmly that the whole cave shone gold.


    She had learned something,
    those long summer evenings. A light isn''t only for the lost. It is for whoever
    is drawn to it.


    And somebody always is, in the end.'
  - 'I should tell you: that fox spirit was me.


    Rosa grew up, and grew old,
    and her grandchildren still bring me bread and honey on midsummer''s eve. My
    lantern still hangs at the mouth of the cave.


    And if you are reading this,
    then you have found me too, which means you followed the light. Come and sit
    by it a while.'
